Checking StyleBro's rules, xunit.analyzers' assertion rules where they are warnings or errors and the built-in rules init and migrate turn on (161 ids), changing nothing; --all checks every analyzer's and compiler fix too.
Indentation kept: Dapper/SqlMapper.cs:3991-4058: '#if' decides whether these lines are an if/else (or loop) body, inside a block, or (a '#region' line) compiled at all, so each framework or configuration would indent them its own way. Their indentation is left as it is.
Held back: IDE0049: this .NET SDK's 'dotnet format' ignores the suppressor StyleBro's multi-target guard uses for IDE rules, so their fixes could write C# or APIs that another target framework or project compiling the same file doesn't have. They aren't applied here; a newer .NET SDK's 'dotnet format' applies them where the guard allows.
Multi-targeted: one 'dotnet format' run per target framework (net10.0, net461, net472, net481, net5.0, net8.0, netstandard2.0). net10.0: only the projects whose code compiles differently there.
Configurations: runs for Release (Dapper, Dapper.StrongName, Dapper.Tests.Performance) too: their code compiles differently there ('#if DEBUG', files or docs of that configuration), and the build of every configuration gets the same fixes and checks.
Shared files: these projects compile the same files with other '#if' symbols or settings, so each group gets a run of its own (loaded together, 'dotnet format' crashes: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/86001): Dapper.EntityFramework.StrongName, Dapper.StrongName | Dapper, Dapper.EntityFramework; Dapper | Dapper.StrongName; Dapper.StrongName | Dapper.
